userStories.description:
**Goal** — Create and export `withNoProgressBail`, then compose it with the existing count bail in the production rectification cycle.

**Motivation** — Finding identity is never compared, so a cycle that returns the same defect at a constant count never trips it and runs to the attempt cap.

**Approach** — The no-progress test operates on `Iteration.findingsBefore` / `Iteration.findingsAfter`, which are full `Finding[]` arrays on the stored iteration (they carry counts only in the *emitted log record* — see #1496). Keys come from the existing `findingKey` helper.

```typescript
// src/execution/story-orchestrator/no-progress-bail.ts

/** True when the iteration resolved nothing: every key present before is still present after. */
function madeNoProgress(iteration: Iteration<Finding>): boolean {
  if (iteration.findingsBefore.length === 0) return false; // nothing to resolve — not a stall
  const after = new Set(iteration.findingsAfter.map(findingKey));
  return iteration.findingsBefore.every((f) => after.has(findingKey(f)));
}

export function withNoProgressBail(
  strategies: FixStrategy<Finding, unknown, unknown, unknown>[],
  enabled: boolean,
  consecutiveNoProgress: number,
): FixStrategy<Finding, unknown, unknown, unknown>[] { /* ... */ }
```

Pattern to mirror: `withIncreasingFailuresBail` — same wrapper signature, same "user-supplied `bailWhen` wins" composition, same early return of the unmodified array when disabled. The new module is a sibling file rather than an addition to `run-phase.ts` (452 lines) or `cycle.ts` (584 lines, already against the 600-line source limit), following the `cycle-iteration-log.ts` precedent from #1496.

Compose the two wrappers so their observable precedence is explicit: a user-supplied predicate wins, then no-progress, then count-increase. The no-progress result must be evaluated ahead of the count-increase result when both predicates match; calling an already count-wrapped `bailWhen` first would violate that requirement. No-progress outranks count-increase because it is the more specific diagnosis — an iteration can satisfy both, and the operator-facing reason should name the stall, not the count.

**Scope** — In: the new bail module, its story-orchestrator and public execution-barrel re-exports, rectification composition, and unit/integration coverage of both the wrapper and production entry point. Out: US-001 owns the config fields; `withIncreasingFailuresBail`, `classifyOutcome`, and the verification-lane rectification consumer remain unchanged.

**Failure handling** — `abortOnNoProgress: false` returns the strategy array unmodified; fewer iterations than the threshold returns `null`; an empty `findingsBefore` is progress; a non-null user `bailWhen` reason is returned unchanged without consulting the predicate; and simultaneous no-progress/count-increase returns the no-progress reason. The bail reason is `no finding resolved for 3 consecutive iteration(s); 2 finding(s) persisted` in the corresponding case. Exits retain `exitReason: "bail-when"` and carry the string in `FixCycleResult.bailDetail`; production observability is the number of validation/fix-operation runs, not a new `RectificationResult` field.
